     "source": "",
     "task": "In namespace `arch-a` (create it if it doesn't exist), create a pod named `webpod` using image `nginx:1.27`. The container must be named `webcont`, declare `containerPort: 80`, and the pod must carry the labels `type=webserver` and `tier=front`.",
     "solution": "```bash\nk create ns arch-a\nk -n arch-a run webpod --image=nginx:1.27 --port=80 --labels=type=webserver,tier=front $do > 2.1.yaml\n# rename the container from \"webpod\" to \"webcont\": the generator writes \"- image:\" first,\n# so the container's name is the indented line below it (metadata.name stays webpod)\nsed -i 's/^    name: webpod$/    name: webcont/' 2.1.yaml   # or edit in vim\nk apply -f 2.1.yaml\n```\n\nResulting manifest:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: webpod\n  namespace: arch-a\n  labels:\n    type: webserver\n    tier: front\nspec:\n  containers:\n  - name: webcont\n    image: nginx:1.27\n    ports:\n    - containerPort: 80\n```",
     "verify": "```bash\nk -n arch-a get pod webpod --show-labels -o wide\nk -n arch-a get pod webpod -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].name}{\"\\n\"}'\n```",
     "hints": [
      "`kubectl run` can generate the pod for you, including the port and the labels, so start from a dry-run YAML and create the namespace first.",
      "The generated container is named after the pod (`webpod`), so rename it to `webcont` in the YAML; `--port` becomes `containerPort` and `--labels` takes a comma-separated list."
     ],
     "explanation": "**What this trains:** Creating a pod from a generator and fixing the one thing the generator can't set, the container name. Most pod tasks start this way.\n\n**Why the solution looks like this:**\n- `--port` only writes `containerPort: 80` into the spec; it creates no Service. `--labels` sets exactly the two labels the task asks for.\n- `k run` has no flag for the container name, which defaults to the pod name. That is why the manifest goes through `$do` into a file instead of straight to the cluster.\n- kubectl generators write container fields alphabetically: `- image:` comes first and `name:` is the next line, indented four spaces. The sed pattern matches those four spaces, so it renames the container and leaves `metadata.name` (two spaces) as `webpod`.\n- A container name can't be changed on a running pod, so it has to be right before `apply`.\n- `get pod` doesn't show container names; the jsonpath line does.\n\n**On the exam:** Generate with `$do`, fix the name in vim, apply. Check the labels with `--show-labels` and the container name before moving on.\n\n**Docs:**\n- [kubectl run](https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_run/): every flag, including `--port` and `--labels`. Search: `kubectl run`\n- [Pods](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/): a minimal pod manifest to compare with. Search: `pods`",
