     "id": "2.5",
     "title": "Native sidecar container",
     "minutes": 5,
     "source": "Curriculum: multi-container design. Native sidecars (`restartPolicy: Always` on an init container) are stable since Kubernetes v1.33.",
     "task": "In namespace `arch-b`, create a pod `native-sidecar` where:\n- an init container `log-shipper` (`busybox:1.36`) runs for the whole life of the pod as a **native sidecar**, running `sh -c 'tail -F /var/log/app/app.log'`\n- the main container `app` (`busybox:1.36`) runs `sh -c 'while true; do echo \"$(date) hello\" >> /var/log/app/app.log; sleep 5; done'`\n- both share an `emptyDir` volume `logs` mounted at `/var/log/app`",
     "solution": "```yaml\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata:\n  name: native-sidecar\n  namespace: arch-b\nspec:\n  volumes:\n  - name: logs\n    emptyDir: {}\n  initContainers:\n  - name: log-shipper\n    image: busybox:1.36\n    restartPolicy: Always          # this makes it a native sidecar\n    command: [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"tail -F /var/log/app/app.log\"]\n    volumeMounts:\n    - name: logs\n      mountPath: /var/log/app\n  containers:\n  - name: app\n    image: busybox:1.36\n    command: [\"sh\", \"-c\", \"while true; do echo \\\"$(date) hello\\\" >> /var/log/app/app.log; sleep 5; done\"]\n    volumeMounts:\n    - name: logs\n      mountPath: /var/log/app\n```",
     "verify": "```bash\nk -n arch-b get pod native-sidecar        # READY 2/2\nk -n arch-b logs native-sidecar -c log-shipper --tail=3\n```",
     "hints": [
      "A native sidecar is an init container that keeps running alongside the main containers, and both containers share an `emptyDir` volume.",
      "Put `restartPolicy: Always` on the entry under `initContainers` to make it a sidecar, and mount the `logs` volume at `/var/log/app` in both containers; mind the escaped quotes in the app command."
     ],
     "explanation": "**What this trains:** The native sidecar: a helper that starts before the app and runs for the whole life of the pod. It is stable since Kubernetes v1.33.\n\n**Why the solution looks like this:**\n- A native sidecar is an init container with `restartPolicy: Always`. That one field tells the kubelet not to wait for the container to finish: it starts it, then moves on to `app`.\n- Without the field, `tail -F` is an ordinary init container that never completes, and `app` never starts.\n- The sidecar starts first, so `app.log` doesn't exist yet. `tail -F` (capital F) keeps retrying until the file appears; `tail -f` would fail on the missing file.\n- An `emptyDir` mounted in both containers is how they see the same file. It lives as long as the pod.\n- READY shows `2/2`: a sidecar counts as a running container, which a normal init container never does.\n\n**On the exam:** No generator writes `initContainers`, so generate a pod with `$do` and add the volume and the init block by hand. Check `2/2` and that the sidecar's log shows the app's lines.\n\n**Docs:**\n- [Sidecar Containers](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/sidecar-containers/): a full example and the start and stop order. Search: `sidecar containers`\n- [Kubernetes v1.33 release](https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/04/23/kubernetes-v1-33-release/#stable-sidecar-containers): the graduation to stable. Search: `v1.33 sidecar containers`",
